e\n\nNear rows 5–8, columns 14–17 of pair 3, the 0s form a block bigger than 3×3, so two all-zero windows exist there:\n\n- **First window, starting at (5,14):** rows 5–7, columns 14–16.\n- **Second window, starting at (6,15):** rows 6–8, columns 15–17.\n\nMy program painted both. The wrong cells are (6,17), (7,17), (8,15), (8,16) and (8,17). Those are exactly the second window's cells that lie outside the first. The expected output keeps only the first window.\n\nSo the corrected rule is: scan positions in reading order (top to bottom, then left to right). Accept a fully-0 3×3 window only if it does not overlap a window already accepted. Paint each accepted window with 1.\n\nEffect of the correction on each pair:\n- **Pair 3:** the window at (5,14) is accepted. The window at (6,15) overlaps it and is skipped. The square at rows 16–18, columns 3–5 is unaffected.\n- **Pairs 1 and 2:** they already matched, and each has only one all-zero window, so nothing changes there.\n\n## Test input\n\nThe same greedy scan runs on the test grid (background 4, noise colour 0). The program emits one stage per accepted square and then the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    cols = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg]\n    noise = cols[0] if cols else 0\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    taken = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    for y in range(H - 2):\n        for x in range(W - 2):\n            if np.all(g[y:y+3, x:x+3] == noise) and not taken[y:y+3, x:x+3].any():\n                taken[y:y+3, x:x+3] = True\n                out[y:y+3, x:x+3] = 1\n                emit(out, f\"The 3x3 window at rows {y}-{y+2}, cols {x}-{x+2} is entirely colour {noise} and overlaps no earlier square; fill it with 1.\")\n    emit(out, \"All non-overlapping solid 3x3 blocks of the noise colour (taken in reading order) are filled with 1; final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the corrected rule is: scan positions in reading order (top to bottom, then left to right)."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"All non-overlapping solid 3x3 blocks of the noise colour (taken in reading order) are filled with 1; final grid."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The window at (6,15) overlaps it and is skipped."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"The 3x3 window at rows 6-8, cols 3-5 is entirely colour 0 and overlaps no earlier square; fill it with 1."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"All non-overlapping solid 3x3 blocks of the noise colour (taken in reading order) are filled with 1; final grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0fe6c9b880371d933fc28f225a93d7917aaa3148a220aac825d1b2f4e5836650","task_sha256":"ec17663f69d35bb1a60510de9eba9632cb4a18373b2c85409ee52aff18c19bd8","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
